WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Speaker 1: Welcome back to The David Pakman Show,&nbsp;
I'm producer Pat Forde here, filling in for David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.880
on this Christmas Eve. David is traveling right&nbsp;
now. He's on vacation in Southern California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.720
having a good time. And he is going to&nbsp;
be back with us on Monday. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.280
it's been a little while since we gave a COVID&nbsp;
update on the show, and I thought that given the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.760
latest surge that we're seeing in infections,&nbsp;
it would be good to provide you with one today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.520
Obviously, much of this latest surge&nbsp;
has to do with the al-Muqrin variant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:31.440
which we first heard about only a month ago. Hard&nbsp;
to believe. And since it came from South Africa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.480
in such a short amount of time, it has been able&nbsp;
to become the dominant strain here in the U.S.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.920
with an estimated 70 to 80 percent of current&nbsp;
cases being attributed to the variant. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.880
the speculation when it comes to Macron is that&nbsp;
it's a lot more contagious, yes, but not nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.160
as severe that less severe part we're not totally&nbsp;
sure about. We'll know for sure. Hopefully, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.000
next couple of weeks or so, I'm going to talk&nbsp;
more about that later. But let's take a look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.080
the case chart. Looking at the case chart back in&nbsp;
the spring of 2020, we had what looks like a tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:07.280
wave there, which is actually deceptive because&nbsp;
it was hard to come by tests during that time. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.000
numbers were probably a lot more crazy than the&nbsp;
graph shows. But of course, these are confirmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.640
cases. We had this wave in the spring of 2020, and&nbsp;
then we got a small wave in the summer of 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.880
mainly because of places like Texas and Florida.&nbsp;
There are places like New England at that time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.720
that were doing just fine, but there are other&nbsp;
parts of the country not doing so well in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:33.840
summer of 2020. Then by the winter of 2020 into&nbsp;
2021, we had this just out of control giant spike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:40.960
topping out at about 250000 to 300000 cases per&nbsp;
day. But then, as the weather warmed up and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:46.320
importantly, as people got vaccinated, because&nbsp;
this is the time that vaccines became available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.720
we went back to super low numbers and many of us&nbsp;
thought that in May, June, July of this year that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:58.640
this all could be over with soon. But then we got&nbsp;
into the late summer. There was another wave then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.400
which calmed down by October. And&nbsp;
now unfortunately, we're heading back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:08.960
up seeing about 150000 ish new cases a day. And&nbsp;
the question is, how high are we going to go?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.160
Retiring now, director Francis Collins is&nbsp;
predicting that things could get as bad here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:22.640
as one million new cases a day. One million. And&nbsp;
that's just based on how contagious al-Muqrin is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.520
now. Looking at the death numbers, we're seeing&nbsp;
a slight uptick so far from the October Valley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:34.160
getting around 1500 deaths a day. But as we&nbsp;
know, deaths tend to lag cases by a few weeks. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:41.200
are we not seeing a major surge yet in the death&nbsp;
numbers because Omicron isn't nearly as deadly? Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.920
is it because just not enough time has passed yet&nbsp;
and because people are getting infected with it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:51.360
now? We could possibly see the surge in deaths in&nbsp;
the next couple of weeks. We just don't know yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.960
Another key point is that even if we do find&nbsp;
al-Muqrin to be less deadly than previous strains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:01.120
that's not going to help us all that much. If&nbsp;
we're seeing a million cases a day, like even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.840
if the rate is somewhat low, if we're seeing that&nbsp;
many cases, there's still going to be thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.400
and thousands of people who die from this every&nbsp;
single day. And it could be just as bad overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:17.040
as the big wave that we saw in the winter of last&nbsp;
year. So that's one important thing to consider.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.360
Now, obviously, we know that vaccination is&nbsp;
our best way to protect against infection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.720
but especially against severe COVID and death.&nbsp;
And that's why some cities are joining cities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.840
like New York and Los Angeles and now requiring&nbsp;
proof of vaccination to do indoor activities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:37.360
And also because of Macron, we're seeing some&nbsp;
cancelations that we arguably haven't seen since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.640
the start of the pandemic. So let me run through&nbsp;
you a list of cancelations that we've seen over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.520
the past week or so, and I'm sure that this&nbsp;
list is going to be added to over the coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.960
days. Broadway shows have now closed their doors&nbsp;
through Christmas week. The ball drop in Times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.840
Square will go on, but it's going to be scaled&nbsp;
back with masks required for those in attendance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.200
The World Economic Forum, scheduled for Davos,&nbsp;
Switzerland, has been postponed until the summer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.400
I'm sure most many other conferences will&nbsp;
follow soon. Companies shutting their doors to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:14.960
non-essential workers include CNN, Apple, Google,&nbsp;
Uber, Lyft and Ford. Universities that shut down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.640
their campuses early for the remainder of the&nbsp;
semester included Cornell, NYU and Princeton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.440
And then in terms of travel, Israel, France&nbsp;
and the Netherlands closed their borders to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.040
more countries and added more travel restrictions&nbsp;
when it comes to sports, the NFL decided to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:36.560
some games around the NBA, decided to postpone a&nbsp;
few of their games in the NHL as maybe being the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:42.080
most strict. They decided to put a pause on games&nbsp;
until after Christmas, and also they've decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:47.520
pull out of the Winter Olympics in Beijing, China,&nbsp;
because of the surge. So we'll have to wait and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.280
see whether or not these decisions make sense.&nbsp;
I think the sentiment is, well, you'd rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:57.200
be safe than sorry. Let's see if cases reach a&nbsp;
peak any time soon, and if they do reach a peak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.920
let's see where that peak exactly is. But the&nbsp;
numbers right now, they're just not going in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.720
right direction, and what worries us, especially&nbsp;
now, is that we're about to see families get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.400
together for Christmas. We're about to see friends&nbsp;
get together for New Year's celebrations. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.920
there's definitely a whole lot of reasons to be&nbsp;
cautious now about Omicron and COVID in general.

